WHAT TO WRITE

1. "headline" + "turn": two short lines that together state the finding. The first corrects what they believed; the second says what is really true. Plain words, no jargon, no numbers, each under 40 characters.
   Good: "You're not short of clients." / "You're full."
   Good: "Your ads work." / "Your follow-up doesn't."
   Good: "The second clinic can work." / "Not at your current prices."
   If we agree with what they came in thinking, say so plainly: "You were right." / "Evenings are where you lose them."

2. "sub": two or three sentences that give the evidence in their own figures and end on what that means for what they asked for. Every number must be one of the figures you may use. When a figure marked as worked out settles the question, that is your evidence: lead with it.
   "emphasis": a short phrase copied EXACTLY from your sub that carries the finding — it will be set in bold. Or "".

3. "figures": up to two headline figures that prove it, each a short "value" (e.g. "$50 vs $100", "10 hrs") and a "label" under it (e.g. "you, and the comparable studios nearby"), with the claim ids it comes from in "cites". Every number in value and label must be one of the figures you may use. Do not repeat a capacity total — the page draws that already.

4. "move": the value of doing the thing we recommend, as a product of terms, or null when their figures cannot support one. Each term is one factor:
     {"label": "evening spots a week", "value": 100, "from": "CAP-05"}          <- one of the claims above, by its claim id
     {"label": "weeks a year", "value": 52, "from": "constant"}               <- only calendar constants
     {"label": "price rise per spot", "value": 50, "parts": [
         {"value": 100, "from": "proposed", "label": "proposed evening price"},
         {"value": 50, "from": "CF-04", "sign": -1}]}                          <- a difference; "proposed" is OUR number and will be labelled as ours
   "label" is the move in a few words with its condition, e.g. "moving the busy evening slots to $100, if regulars stay".
   "unit" is "USD", "hours" or the unit the product comes out in. "period" is "year", "month" or "week".
   Use a "proposed" number only where the move needs one (a new price, a new slot). Never propose a number about the business as it is today. If the value would rest mostly on proposed numbers, return null — a guess dressed as a figure is worse than no figure.

5. "action": what the owner can start WITHOUT software, as the button that starts it.
   {"weeks": 4, "name": "pricing pilot"} for a trial they run and measure; {"weeks": 8, "name": "intake plan"} when it is steps rather than a trial. Keep every number out of the name (a name with one is replaced by "N-week plan", from "weeks"). Weeks between 2 and 12.

